sentences.l:
O my-friend, I have forsaken my-sleep, * and increased my-anguish for the distance of-my-lands.
A father, was to-me toward me compassionate, * he-departed from-me and neighboured the graves;
then-came after him upon me affairs * I-became by-them a-shatterer of-the livers.
He-bought for-me, of the slave-girls, a-fair-maid * her-figure, the swaying, shames the bough:
so-I-spent what I-inherited upon her, * and I-was-lavish with-it upon the open-handed.
I-offered-her the-sale when increased my-anguish, * and the-ache of-the-parting was not by-my-wish;
and-when a-crier called to-her, * there-added for-her a-shaykh, abundant of-corruption.
So for-this I-was-enraged, a-raging severe, * and I-snatched her-hand from the-hand of-the-base-ones;
so-he-punched-me, this the-vile-one, in-rage, * then blazed in-him the-flame of-impiety.
So-I-punched-him from my-burning with-my-right * and my-left, until I-healed my-heart.
And from the-fear I-came to-my-house, * and-hid, from-dread of-the-adversaries;
so-commanded the-ruler of-the-land to-seize-me, * and-came the-chamberlain, the abundant in-rectitude,
signalling to-me that I go far * and-vanish from-them and-grieve the-envious.
So-we-went-out from our-house at-the-dark of-night, * seeking the-abiding in Baghdad.
There-is-not a-thing of the-treasures with-me * I-give-you other-than what I-gave, O fisherman;
except that I-give-you the-beloved of-my-heart: * so be-certain that I-have-given my-heart.
